====================================================================================================
PART E -- THE BTFR.  Deep-MOND V^4 = G M a0 zero-point and scatter under the exponential kernel.
====================================================================================================

      BTFR sample: 139 galaxies with a flat V_flat (Q<3, inc>=30 deg); M/L fixed at 0.5
      free-slope BTFR: slope alpha = 3.387 (deep-MOND predicts 4.00; sub-4 here is a fixed-M/L + coarse-V_flat regression effect, not a kernel property), scatter 0.219 dex
      footing    implied a0 [m/s^2]  log d vs footing  zero-pt offset(dex)   rms(dex)
      canonical           1.406e-10            +0.177               -0.177      0.324
      alt                 1.406e-10            +0.096               -0.096      0.286
      the data's own deep-MOND normalisation a0 = 1.406e-10 m/s^2 sits +0.177 dex from canonical and +0.096 dex from alt
      exp-vs-nu_RAR difference over the BTFR flat points: worst-case 0.0726 dex; even over the 92 deep-MOND (g_bar<0.3a0) galaxies it is 0.0476 dex (they converge only as y->0)
      but the BTFR scatter is 0.219 dex, so this 0.073 dex kernel difference is buried below the noise
  [PASS] E1  a MOND-like power-law BTFR exists under the kernel (slope 3.0 <= alpha <= 4.5)   (alpha = 3.387)
  [PASS] E2  the deep-MOND BTFR zero-point recovers a0 to within 0.25 dex of BOTH footings   (implied a0 = 1.406e-10; +0.177/+0.096 dex)
  [PASS] E3  the BTFR cannot discriminate the kernels: their max BTFR difference is well below the scatter   (kernel difference 0.073 dex vs BTFR scatter 0.219 dex (asymptote is identical, A4))

====================================================================================================
PART F -- VERDICT.
====================================================================================================

      FOUNDATIONAL QUESTION: does the F(Q)Theta exponential kernel FIT galaxies?  YES -- with the
      CORRECT (AQUAL) reading it delivers a MOND-like RAR at ZERO per-galaxy parameters:
          exponential kernel:  rms 0.1613 / 0.1507 dex   (medians +0.075 / +0.047)
          nu_RAR (McGaugh):    rms 0.1453 / 0.1421 dex
          carried boundedboost:rms 0.1453 / 0.1421 dex   (= saturated nu_RAR)
      RANKING: exponential is WORSE than nu_RAR by +0.0160/+0.0086 dex -- inside the
      ~0.01-0.02 'comparable' band, so it FITS, but it is the poorest of the three MOND kernels tested.
      BTFR: MOND-like power law (slope 3.39; sub-4 from fixed M/L + coarse V_flat, not the kernel), scatter 0.22 dex,
      deep-MOND zero-point a0 = 1.41e-10 m/s^2 (within 0.10 dex of alt, 0.18 of canonical).
      The exponential and nu_RAR kernels give the SAME BTFR (identical deep-MOND limit), so galaxies do NOT
      decide the D1 kernel conflict -- the external-field / wide-binary scale (L89) does.
      The naive g_bar-variable reading (g_obs=g_bar/(1-e^{-y})) is deep-MOND-broken and must never be used.

